00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:07.870 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  one thing we've learned in our work is  that human beings have a tendency to see  patterns make inferences and judgments  and draw conclusions quite quickly from  just a little bit of data this is an  important skill it allows us to make  thoughtful inferences and predictions  but it can also lead to bad or  misinformed judgments when we're too  quick to draw inferences from a small  amount of data in our work with schools  we have found that a mental model called  the ladder of inference is especially  useful in helping educators resist this  tendency and stay focused on the  evidence the ladder of inference was  first developed by business theorist  Chris arduous and popularized by Peter  Sangay and colleagues in a book called  the fifth discipline the art and  practice of the learning organization  the ladder of inference is a metaphor  organized around how we as humans tend  to make patterns about what we see in  the world  whenever I perceive something I first  select some data to focus on then I add  some interpretation draw conclusions and  finally take actions the actions I take  then influence what kind of data I  collect next and for that reason it can  be very dangerous to go up the ladder  too quickly at data wise we like to use  this model to help each other maintain a  relentless focus on evidence when we  hear somebody add interpretations or  draw conclusions too quickly we try to  use phrases like that statement is  pretty high up on the ladder what  evidence are you using or we're all  making a lot of interpretations here  let's try to go back down that ladder  and talk first about the evidence there  might be a good instructional suggestion  but let's stick with maybe what we  wonder about what this is I'm King I  jumped us on the ladder for example  imagine an administrator pops into a  classroom and notices that there's lots  of students chatting at once but the  teacher is over in the corner paying  attention to just one student if the  administrator adds the interpretation  that the student is distracting the  teacher from his work and that the other  children are taking advantage of the  situation and just fooling around the  administrator might draw the conclusion  that the teacher has cost room  management problems and then put a  negative note in the teacher's file in  that example the administrator jumped  from her observation that multiple  students were talking at once and that  the teacher was only talking to one  student both of which were true facts  but she interpreted that the teacher was  distracted and that the students were  off task not taking the time to  investigate whether other  interpretations could have been equally  plausible for example maybe the teacher  was deliberately giving one-on-one  feedback to each student that day and  the rest of the students were supposed  to be engaged in small group discussion  by using the ladder of inference to help  you note when you're making inferences  you can catch yourself every time you  treat an interpretation as though it  were a fact because this discipline can  be so helpful this course will give you  lots of practice in starting low on the  ladder and making a conscious choice  whenever you want to climb up it for  each step of the data wise course you'll  have a chance to look at an artifact  from a school and make observations that  are grounded in the data a very helpful  way of sticking to evidence that data  wise schools use is a protocol called I  notice I wonder first we focus on  statements about what we notice these  statements should be as specific and  non-judgmental as possible then we  wonder about what we noticed these  wonderings start to climb up the ladder  to include interpretations of the data  but they're framed as questions since we  are less certain of them wonderings  allow us to ask meaningful questions  about the evidence while also providing  a space to let us acknowledge the  inferences that our minds may be jumping  to you will then have a chance to see  how members of the data wise team  collaboratively notice and wonder when  making sense of a wide range of data  sources this exercise will help you  cultivate the e  the ACE habits of mind a relentless  focus on evidence  [Music]

